  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 312 PM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nCurrent visible satellite imagery shows mostly clear skies across \nthe U.P. this afternoon. Temperatures are in the upper 50s near the \nlakeshore and low 60s in the southern portion of the forecast area. \nAn area of surface high pressure has been building throughout the \nday and is expected to continue strengthening as it moves eastward. \nAt the upper levels, a 500mb ridge is amplifying over the Plains as \na trough moves off the coast of northern Canada.\n\n500mb ridging will continue to amplify tonight into Saturday. At the \nsurface, WAA will increase overnight into Saturday as winds \nstrengthen and shift southerly in response to the building high \npressure to the east and deepening low to the west. This influx of \nwarm air will keep lows mainly in the 40s overnight. A bit of cloud \ncover moves in overnight with a very slight chance of some sprinkles \nbefore skies clear out Saturday morning. Gusty southerly winds \ncontinue to strengthen with a tightening surface pressure gradient \nduring the day Saturday. Most areas could see gusts up to 30mph, \nwhile areas prone to downsloping could see some gusts upwards of \n40mph. Highs are expected to reach the low 80s (15-30 degrees higher \nthan normal) in the western U.P. while the east will remain in the \nmid-70s due to the moderation of temperature as winds move across \nthe relatively cool surface of Lake Michigan. Afternoon RHs are \nexpected to fall to around 40% in the western U.P., remaining in the \n50s for the east. Under these conditions, elevated to critical \nweather conditions are expected for the western U.P. Saturday. \n\nWarm temperatures, gusty winds, and low RHs continue into Sunday. \nHighs will return to the low 80s in the west (mid 70s in the east) \nSunday afternoon, while RHs fall into mid-30s (mid-40s in the east). \nWind speeds are expected to reduce to 15-25mph heading into Sunday \nafternoon as the surface pressure gradient from Saturday weakens. \nClear skies are expected to continue until Sunday evening. Heading \ninto the work week, the ridging pattern aloft flattens out somewhat \nunder the influence of troughing over northern Ontario. At the \nsurface, troughing associated with a frontal system is expected to \nmove across the area. This system will bring chances for \nprecipitation Monday afternoon through Tuesday morning. Showers are \nexpected to be widespread but scattered with low accumulations, with \na ~50% chance for precipitation totals to exceed 0.05 inches by \nTuesday morning at any given location. Higher accumulations are \npossible along the lakeshore as northerly winds behind the cold \nfront allow for the possibility of lake enhancement. The passage of \nthe remnants of Isaias through the eastern CONUS this weekend \nintroduces some uncertainty in the models for next week. Currently, \nupper-level ridging is expected to return for the midweek, bringing \nback seasonable temperatures and light winds before a troughing \npattern looks to move through Thursday evening.",
